ta  integrity by reducing the opportunities  for human error  the three quality control procedures  discussed in this presentation  form a common thread in the  biopharmaceutical manufacturing industry  and are water quality testing clean room  routine  environmental monitoring and final  injectable drug particulate  testing  so first let's take a look at quality  control testing  and how this can be open to human error  unfortunately resulting in data  integrity  issues  now in 2018 december of 2018 the fda  published their questions and answers  paper  relating to 21 cfr part 11 data  integrity  and they shared some fairly recent uh  sorry fairly worrying  observations they stated that in recent  years they'd increasingly observed cgmp  violations particularly involving data  integrity  during their inspections and they said  that these violations have led to  numerous regulatory  actions including warning letters import  alerts and consent degrees  and they go on to say that firms should  implement meaningful and effective  strategies to manage their data  integrity risks and what they're  suggesting is that you should make  processes more  robust  also in their 2018 guidance the fda  introduced the acronym for the first  time  alcoa and this is in response to a  what's what a good electronic record  should look like and  they say the the acronym stands for  attributable to the person taking the  sample  they suggest it should be legible and  therefore possible probably not  handwritten  sometimes handwriting can be difficult  to read  they should they say it should be made  contemporaneously which means  at the time of the test so the  electronic record should be made  at the time of the test and rather than  later and i they're referring there  really  to the common practice of taking paper  printouts from test machines  and then manually typing that  information in to create the electronic  record at a later time  and they're saying that's not good  practice and they also say of course the  electronic record  should be the original one and the data  it contains  should be accurate and it's that last  accurate a for accurate that i'm going  to  focus on today  now in a typical manual sampling  and data quality control uh situation  there's many  manuals as steps in the sop and first  the technician first of all of course  technician has to make sure  they're using the correct version of the  sop  and then they have to manually configure  the instrument to sample for the correct  volume and the correct number of samples  according to the sop for each different  test  next they print the sample results out  and hand write the comments onto the  printer  printout and sign it with their name to  make it attributable  and then at the end of each day they  manually type the results  in to create the electronic record from  all these pieces of paper they've  created  and finally the electronic record has to  be reviewed by the supervisor  a to ensure that all the samples are  being taken correctly and b  that the data in the electronic record  matches the paper printouts  now as you can see this is a very manual  process  and with thousands of samples pieces of  paper and manual data transcriptions  every month  the opportunity for human error to creep  in and impact the integrity of the data  or the accuracy of the data very high  indeed  so i wanted to share with you a study  which is done by the industrial  biodevelopment  laboratory and they showed in a test  between  16 experienced qc technicians performing  the same  manual sop standard operating procedure  a variability of up to 20 between the  results  now you could argue that the manual sop  could be improved and the technicians  retrained  but such variability and results does  suggest that manual processes  are arguably not robust enough to  deliver the required  levels of quality control  now whilst the technical specifications  of similar instrumentation may appear  equivalent when looking to improve  quality control  the qc team should really be looking at  the level of automation that the  instrument offers  to make qc testing more robust and  repeatable  for example a common source of errors in  qc testing is sample preparation so  any instrument that alternates this  process can enhance the reproducibility  and the robustness of a qc test  so let's take a look at our first  quality control test  the quality control testing for purified  water  and water for injection and in fact  in this talk here i'll talk about how we  can use online instrumentation that  automate this testing  and use that data from them to help  release batch  files  now total organic carbon or toc and  conductivity are two  of the four critical water quality  attributes defined for water injection  and purified water  in the united states pharmacopoeia now  online analyzers such as the pat 700  from batman culture  can be validated to comply fully with  both of these  key quality attributes according to the  pharmacopeel  requirements  the pharmacopoeia is asked that toc is  measured by first measuring the  inorganic material present in the sample  total inorganic carbon they call this or  tic  and the pharmacopoeia's guide us that  tic's mainly dissolved carbon dioxide in  purified water and water injection  the pharmacopoeia is then asked that the  organic material present in the sample  is now  completely oxidized to release the  organic carbon in the form of carbon  dioxide  the carbon dioxide from the organic or  sorry oxidized organic material  and the inorganic carbon dioxide now add  together to represent the total carbon  present in the water or tc  and then the pharmacopoeia is asked that  we subtract the tic  from the tc to calculate the total  organic carbon or toc now this is a  graphical representation of the  oxidization process initially the  conductivity of the water sample is  measured to establish the amount of tic  present in the sample which as i said is  made up of carbon dioxide dissolved in  the water  then an ultraviolet lamp is used to  oxidize all the organic material in the  sample to carbon dioxide  and when there's no further changes in  the conductivity we know we've completed  that  oxidation and the analyzer measures the  new conductivity value which is the  total carbon or tc  and then it subtracts the initial  conductivity measurement the tic from  the tc  and the difference in this is directly  proportional to the toc  or in the water sample itself  now traditionally data from online toc  analyzers is fed straight to the site's  supervisory control  or data acquisition system or  distributed control systems scada or dcs  and making access to this data for qc  team difficult  however the p8700 can be configured to  automatically  send pdf electronic records every day  over the ethernet for review and batch  release  and this satisfies the fda's alcoa  requirement for electronic records to be  accurate  legible contemporaneously created  original  and attributable  now unlike laboratory waters quality  testing  instruments which utilize reagents and  require manual configuration for each  sample and frequent  sometimes daily calibration the online  pat 700 from beckmann culture  requires no reagents it automates the  test protocol and only requires  calibration every 12 months  calibration and system suitability  testing sops are automated  requiring no manual data entry and pass  fail reports are automatically generated  from the instrument  eliminating manual calculations  calibration standards once loaded into  the p8700  upload their certified value lot number  and expiry date to the p8700  using radio frequency identification  electronic chips are located on the  calibration bottles  and these are read by the p8700 thus  avoiding manual data entry  okay so let's move on to look what can  be done to automate  routine clean room environmental  monitoring  programs  now there 