  and now there are approximately 35.2  cubic feet of air in one cubic meter  so used users must multiply the counts  results by 35.2 to get the counts per  meter  so you can see the opportunity for error  there now it is true  that most particle counters can be  programmed to automatically make this  calculation  for the user but it's another  configuration step to remember to do to  ensure the results are reported  correctly and i have seen people  not doing that and just writing down the  counts per cubic foot  now as i mentioned the north american  fda cgmp document does differ to the  european and world health organization  guidance  in the only mentions one particle size  the 0.5 micron particles  however of course drug manufacturers  based in north america wanting to sell  their products  outside of north america will have to  count both .5 and 5  microns as they have to be in compliance  with the standards of the industry  and the country to which they want to  export  now as i mentioned a couple of moments  ago both the european  gmp and the north american gmp tell the  reader to refer to the classification  procedures laid down in the iso standard  1 4  6 4 part 1 for classification  now this in itself is not a gmp document  and it's designed to give guidance  rather than mandate  but the fact that it's referenced by the  gmps gives it the same status as a gmp  document  for those manufacturers regulated by gmp  now it has a wide range of clean room  classes as you can see from one  to nine and it covers again a wide range  of particle sizes from 0.5 microns up to  5 microns  and then it defines in the table the  number of particles  for each class you'll notice this is the  2015  version of the iso standard here and for  those of you  with sharp eyes will notice this at iso  class 5  um for 5 microns there's no number in  the table anymore that was removed  in the 2015 version however  um if you're complying to the european  gmp or world health organization  that five micron particles is still in  those regulations so  even though it's gone from the um the  146 44 document  you still have to measure those if  you're complying with european or world  health organization rules  so the idea with the iso standard is the  user must decide  what particle sizes they're interested  in and how clean the clean room must be  and then they choose a subset of the  clean room classes and particle size  from the iso table  now gmp the european version has decided  that particle sizes are interest of 0.5  and 5 microns and you can see from grade  b at rest if we go to the grade b  table here 0.5 and 5  and the number of particles in the table  there are the same  as iso class 5. so you can see how gmp  has taken a subset of the iso  to make the the gmp regulations  and don't forget if you're following  european gmp  or you are following world health  organization  it is 0.5 and 5 microns still  now discussions i've had as i travel the  world and speak to  users of clean rooms in the gmp  environment  is that and there's an increasing trend  where the burden of carrying out these  routine environmental monitoring  testing particularly air particle  counting is moving away from the qc  microbiology team and over to the  production staff for two reasons firstly  the microbiology staff are  very overqualified shall we say to carry  out such simple routine tasks  and the second point is it reduces the  number of people in the clean rooms  thus hopefully reducing the potential  for product contamination  however the downside is the production  team do not  of course have the same high level of  knowledge about microbial and routine  environmental monitoring  and this creates challenges in itself  now routine environmental monitoring is  arguably one of the most manual  and complex processes that we have to do  it involves the collection of thousands  of samples each month  using portable air particle counters  and typically there are many manual sop  steps to follow it in the routine  environmental monitoring program  first of course the technician has to  make sure they're using the correct  version of the paper  sop then they have to follow the uh  something map in the sop and go to every  sample location  then they have to manually type the  location name into the counter via the  on-screen keyboard  and next they have to configure the  counter to sample for the correct amount  of time  correct volume and the number of samples  according to their own sop for each  location  next they print the sample results out  from the counter and handwrite any  comments onto the printout  and then sign it with their name and at  the end of every day they photocopy all  these printouts  and then they manually transcribe the  results from every piece of paper  to create an electronic record then  finally at the end of the day the  electronic record is reviewed by the  supervisor to try and ensure that all  the samples have been taken correctly  and the data in the electronic record  actually matches that of the paper  printouts so  as you can see this is a horrible manual  process and with thousands of samples  pieces of paper manual data  transcriptions every month  the opportunity for human error to creep  in and impact the integrity of the data  is very high  so with the new uh met one thirty four  hundred plus particle counter from  uh beckman cult we've tried to address  these issues with the clarity software  which runs inside the counter itself  first of all improves the accuracy  because you it allows you to upload your  own  sampling map from your routine  environmental monitoring  sop into the counter creating an  interactive version of that sampling map  on the screen of the counter itself  now clarity software includes as i said  the interactive sampling map on the  counter screen  the electronic records are created  contemporaneously and can be exported  via  built-in wi-fi or wired ethernet and  this is standard  it exports these in secure pdf they're  password protected pdf  or you can get it in excel if you want  to trend them  and there's a tab separated variable  format for lims integration  it uses microsoft active directory for  username and password control so it's  very secure  and the exporting the data exporting  function is  ldap which is the currently regarded as  the most secure way of doing this  this time sop version control is done in  the counter itself  and the name of the sop that's been used  on the day  plus the version number appears inside  the electronic record so you have high  traceability  now the counter can work with a usb  barcode reader barcode scanner sorry and  it can scan the  microbial plates used at each location  so they then appear in the  um electronic record as well so that way  you're tying  the settle plate and the active air  plates that you use at each location in  the clean room  to a date and a time so this gives  traceability for those plates  so you can see in the electronic record  you have the plate ids you have the name  of the user  and you have the particle count results  all in one electronic record  so finally let's take a look at the  requirements for particulate testing  in parental drug solutions and see how  these tests themselves can be made  more robust now  the global harmonic appears are  harmonized and the fact that they all  recommend  a primary method for subvisible  particulates  counting in parenteral to be light  obscuration or  sometimes the terms light blocking and  this is the preferred method as i said  for subvisible particle testing  they go on to say that if it's not  possible to use light obscuration for  some  reason for instance if the solution is  too viscous or too dark  then you can use a secondary method  which is the optical  microscope however there's good reason  why light  observation is the preferred preferred  method  and of course the reason for that is  that the contaminating particles we find  in parenterals are not con conveniently  circular  which would allow ease so it would allow  easier and more reproducible counting  and sizing  instead the counting particles using a  microscope is very subjective  and there's there are several methods  aimed at standardizing the sizing of  irregular sayings particles but  unfortunately there's not just one  method  and the methods available include  deciding which is the largest dimension  and measuring that or the alternative  methods of martin's diameter and ferrets  diameter  and the problem is they are not only  subjective but each method gives a  different result  now light observation on the other hand  use the laser light to illuminate a  measurement window  the intensity of the light is measured  by a sensor and turned into an  electrical signal  the counter is calibrated by passing  various sizes of spherical beads through  the counter  partially blocking the laser light and  the resultant change in the electrical  signal can now be said to be equivalent  to each particle's size  and now the sample under test is passed  through the con  through and the contaminating particles  will cause a reduction in the laser  light reaching the sensor  they'll block it or obscure it  and the resultant change in the electric  electrical signal  signal is then compared to those of the  calibrating particles and an equivalent  spherical diameter  can be assigned to the particle now this  method is far more repeatable and it's  non-subjective and that's why they use  light obscuration or recommend that you  should  so let's take a look at the compendium  test methods for subvisible particle  counting  we'll start with usp 788 and ep  2.9.9  the pharma compares here are harmonized  on the required compendium method  and of course it's not possible to test  all the valves in a production batch  because the valves have to be opened to  be tested  so pharmacobears ask as a minimum sample  from each 