ve  challenging as the target for the  sampler is much smaller because there's  not lots of  um vials are pulled into a beaker it's  just a single  um vial and it often has a small conical  base which means it won't stand upright  and you have to have it exactly in the  right place so the sampler  will take the sampler so most particle  counters of course  have a relatively large tear volume  certainly large when compared to 0.2  milliliters  or dead volume sometimes called and this  is the volume that must be pulled into  the counter  before the sample reaches the sensor and  can be counted now  the hiac 9703 sampler can be optimized  for this compendium test by using  the optional small vial clamp holds the  vial upright  and in exactly the right location for  the sampling arm to take the sample  and we have a sampling probe with a very  small tear volume  it's only got 0.091  milliliters tear volume so we can  optimize this test for  for biologics testing  now as i mentioned although largely  harmonized the requirements before  parental drug  particulate testing do vary and  particularly from product to product the  volume of the sample to be analyzed and  the format the results are reported  varies from product to product as i said  in the table so the sampling  requirements for small volume  therapeutic proteins such as vaccines  is different very different to that of a  large volume parental such as  an intravenous drip bag now results must  be calculated and expressed in the  correct format  eg for small volume parentals is counts  per container  for large parenterals like drip bags is  counts per milliliter  so the way the results are expressed  depends on the type  of product you're testing so partial  counters  just do a measurement and deliver  particle counts per milliliter  that leaves the operator then to  calculate the number of particles per  container  or per milliliter and of course that  manual calculation  and interpretation of the results can  i